[{:db/id "q-181733/selection/22ffb6ee0a3d1d9410583977ca4caaeb333a2b4cdca2d2050883bdf03ae685d5" :answer/id #uuid "aac83288-6e9d-58c1-a4fe-1829bdf94b35" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the sets are infinite" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-181733/selection/685c02320dbe687345f93d820499dea4c0c82808b910e8a0e5841f77aa82209d" :answer/id #uuid "57f41e3b-6996-5513-a0b1-0ead33ca562b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-181733/selection/9ed3305a02906a861616af6ff886306c4a2a74885d99f506419451b33c7c9d2d" :answer/id #uuid "e4625fb5-d3f8-5097-9fd2-eb7baa0f296c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-181733/selection/e432f1020975ada4c83798ae75bfd6c0ae4dfe63215763f08b8be8ccabf8a5d3" :answer/id #uuid "95a99d68-5e29-587f-95bb-27d32aa77c49" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-181733/selection/6a47369bccbf2ed1a49e596d056fd7d8dad88dd2a1481ebd8897e05815dcfb4c" :answer/id #uuid "d527f2c5-b039-5849-a01b-a61d514a6452" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$A,B,$ and $C$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-181733/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "36966bc3-5d59-5b84-8ba4-bfd82f50b6ac" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-181733/selection/6a47369bccbf2ed1a49e596d056fd7d8dad88dd2a1481ebd8897e05815dcfb4c" :answer-field/choices ["q-181733/selection/22ffb6ee0a3d1d9410583977ca4caaeb333a2b4cdca2d2050883bdf03ae685d5" "q-181733/selection/685c02320dbe687345f93d820499dea4c0c82808b910e8a0e5841f77aa82209d" "q-181733/selection/9ed3305a02906a861616af6ff886306c4a2a74885d99f506419451b33c7c9d2d" "q-181733/selection/e432f1020975ada4c83798ae75bfd6c0ae4dfe63215763f08b8be8ccabf8a5d3" "q-181733/selection/6a47369bccbf2ed1a49e596d056fd7d8dad88dd2a1481ebd8897e05815dcfb4c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-181733" :question/id #uuid "7cfa83f9-1213-5ba1-a5e4-1a3824447fa9" :question/math-academy-id "q-181733" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,|4n|\\ge 8}$\n- $B={q\\in Q\\,:\\,|4q|\\ge 8}$\n- $C={x\\in R\\,:\\,|4x|\\ge 8}$" :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements. \n\nWe start by solving the following inequality for $x\\in R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|4x|\\ge 8 \\\\ 4|x|\\ge 8 \\\\ |x|\\ge 2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\nx\\le -2\\,\\text{or}\\,x\\ge 2\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x\\in I=(-\\infty ,-2]\\cup [2,\\infty ).$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. If $n\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality $|4n|\\ge 8$ are all the integers that lie in the interval $I.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nA={…,-3,-2}\\cup {2,3,…}.\n$$\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. If $q\\in Q,$ the solutions to $|4q|\\ge 8$ are all the rational numbers that lie in the interval $I.$ Since any real interval of nonzero length contains infinitely many rational numbers, it follows that $B$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Since $B$ is infinite, and $B⊂C,$ it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A,B,$ and $C.$\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-181733/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "efdb77bf-dd8b-5ccc-8265-79bd319afaa3"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-181733"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-181721/selection/2377e3ffc3b5cd65e1036103e0b988e5af6634b2438c63c3b7cc0592571401ab" :answer/id #uuid "87e7d4d2-2abd-5b21-82e8-6232491d9303" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$A$ and $C$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-181721/selection/0980c81a3b754e9d21d74c373c3051efb23b5496ad36e41487147576aca7b00a" :answer/id #uuid "0222a621-f790-5d86-a6a2-c1835cd8fd86" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$A,B,$ and $C$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-181721/selection/e432f1020975ada4c83798ae75bfd6c0ae4dfe63215763f08b8be8ccabf8a5d3" :answer/id #uuid "03293e10-b8ad-5378-8098-b0afbd4df8aa" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-181721/selection/9ed3305a02906a861616af6ff886306c4a2a74885d99f506419451b33c7c9d2d" :answer/id #uuid "3f1503bf-da62-5d80-a0bd-ec60f08d6c3f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-181721/selection/b967c3002b3f913c5b31990d1f8159d3e1e979aa07e2842a8b67bfff1a2fd3f2" :answer/id #uuid "3677b8e2-04ca-5ca9-b637-50d91730f770" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-181721/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "78714006-db71-5818-9b95-53971ed1b6d0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-181721/selection/9ed3305a02906a861616af6ff886306c4a2a74885d99f506419451b33c7c9d2d" :answer-field/choices ["q-181721/selection/2377e3ffc3b5cd65e1036103e0b988e5af6634b2438c63c3b7cc0592571401ab" "q-181721/selection/0980c81a3b754e9d21d74c373c3051efb23b5496ad36e41487147576aca7b00a" "q-181721/selection/e432f1020975ada4c83798ae75bfd6c0ae4dfe63215763f08b8be8ccabf8a5d3" "q-181721/selection/9ed3305a02906a861616af6ff886306c4a2a74885d99f506419451b33c7c9d2d" "q-181721/selection/b967c3002b3f913c5b31990d1f8159d3e1e979aa07e2842a8b67bfff1a2fd3f2"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-181721" :question/id #uuid "5ed4ac5c-82f7-5484-9212-bbc9c754c7f6" :question/math-academy-id "q-181721" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,|5n|\\le 4}$\n- $B={q\\in Q\\,:\\,|5q|\\le 4}$\n- $C={x\\in R\\,:\\,|5x|\\le 4}$" :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\nWe start by solving the following inequality for $x\\in R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|5x|\\le 4 \\\\ 5|x|\\le 4 \\\\ |x|\\le \\frac{4}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives\n\n$$\n-\\frac{4}{5}\\le x\\le \\frac{4}{5}\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x\\in I=[-\\frac{4}{5},\\frac{4}{5}].$ \n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is finite. If $n\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality $|5n|\\le 4$ are all the integers that lie in the interval $I.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\nA={0}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|A|=1,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. If $q\\in Q,$ the solutions to $|5q|\\le 4$ are all the rational numbers that lie in the interval $I.$ Since any real interval of nonzero length contains infinitely many rational numbers, it follows that $B$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Since $B$ is infinite, and $B⊂C,$ it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ and $C$ only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-181721/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "efdb77bf-dd8b-5ccc-8265-79bd319afaa3"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-181721"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-181740"] :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements. \n\nLet's start by analyzing the parabola $y=-{x}^{2}+1$ for $x\\in R.$\n\nFirst, we find the roots of the parabola:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-{x}^{2}+1 & =0 \\\\ -({x}^{2}-1) & =0 \\\\ -(x-1)(x+1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the roots are $x=-1$ and $x=1,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072312/assets/0699524ff20f871c9eeca93faa9934832b8e5816fd23b46862e8b01d48e7d86b.png)\n\nTherefore:\n\n- The solution to the inequality $-{x}^{2}+1>0$ for $x\\in R$ is \n\n$$\n-1<x<1\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x\\in {I}_{1}=(-1,1).$\n\n- Similarly, the solution to the inequality $-{x}^{2}+1<0$ for $x\\in R$ is \n\n$$\nx<-1\\,\\text{or}\\,x>1\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x\\in {I}_{2}=(-\\infty ,-1)\\cup (1,\\infty ).$\n\nWith these in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is finite. If $n\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality $-{n}^{2}+1>0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval ${I}_{1}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nA={0}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|A|=1,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. If $m\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality $-{m}^{2}+1<0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval ${I}_{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nB={…,-3,-2}\\cup {2,3,…}.\n$$\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. If $q\\in Q,$ the solutions to $-{q}^{2}+1<0$ are all the rational numbers that lie in the interval ${I}_{2}.$ Since any real interval of nonzero length contains infinitely many rational numbers, it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ and $C$ only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-181536"] :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements. \n\nLet's start by analyzing the parabola $y={x}^{2}-5x+6$ for $x\\in R.$\n\nFirst, we find the roots of the parabola:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-5x+6 & =0 \\\\ (x-2)(x-3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the roots are $x=2$ and $x=3,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072312/assets/0a42815f626652c332862a38a3812463a9407605329bd81df25f11274b26ce6f.png)\n\nTherefore:\n\n- The solution to the inequality ${x}^{2}-5x+6>0$ for $x\\in R$ is \n\n$$\nx<2\\,\\text{or}\\,x>3\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x\\in {I}_{1}=(-\\infty ,2)\\cup (3,\\infty ).$\n\n- Similarly, the solution to the inequality ${x}^{2}-5x+6<0$ for $x\\in R$ is \n\n$$\n2<x<3\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x\\in {I}_{2}=(2,3).$\n\nWith these in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. If $n\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality ${n}^{2}-5n+6>0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval ${I}_{1}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nA={…,0,1}\\cup {4,5,…}.\n$$\n\n- The set $B$ is finite. If $m\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality ${m}^{2}-5m+6<0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval ${I}_{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nB={}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|B|=0,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. If $q\\in Q,$ the solutions to ${q}^{2}-5q+6<0$ are all the rational numbers that lie in the interval ${I}_{2}.$ Since any real interval of nonzero length contains infinitely many rational numbers, it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A$ and $C$ only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-181768"] :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements. \n\nLet's start by analyzing the parabola $y={x}^{2}-2x$ for $x\\in R.$\n\nFirst, we find the roots of the parabola:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-2x & =0 \\\\ x(x-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the roots are $x=0$ and $x=2,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072312/assets/496c6d53993ca6cae499f617bca42dbc42e4c57bffa278da6312a5b1723bdcbc.png)\n\nTherefore:\n\n- The solution to the inequality ${x}^{2}-2x\\le 0$ for $x\\in R$ is \n\n$$\n0\\le x\\le 2\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x\\in {I}_{1}=[0,2].$\n\n- Similarly, the solution to the inequality ${x}^{2}-2x\\ge 0$ for $x\\in R$ is \n\n$$\nx\\le 0\\,\\text{or}\\,x\\ge 2\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x\\in {I}_{2}=(-\\infty ,0]\\cup [2,\\infty ).$\n\nWith these in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is finite. If $n\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality ${n}^{2}-2n\\le 0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval ${I}_{1}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nA={0,1,2}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|A|=3,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. If $m\\in Z,$ the solutions to the inequality ${m}^{2}-2m\\ge 0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval ${I}_{2}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nB={…,-1,0}\\cup {2,3,…}.\n$$\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. If $q\\in Q,$ the solutions to ${q}^{2}-2q\\le 0$ are all the rational numbers that lie in the interval ${I}_{1}.$ Since any real interval of nonzero length contains infinitely many rational numbers, it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ and $C$ only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-181484/selection/850e7d7e17af59c82782dba2babdfd38f04745309ab27f16b30ef45ba075680e" :answer/id #uuid "eee62ac7-bb06-5be3-8208-73574929ce7c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the sets are infinite." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-181484/selection/e956c3af673c94782038951d8de50bf89c9a53bf4daf2ca6725a4bea4229074c" :answer/id #uuid "fc6d660d-d9fe-5693-8432-01391ffe4b1d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-181484/selection/9ed3305a02906a861616af6ff886306c4a2a74885d99f506419451b33c7c9d2d" :answer/id #uuid "58cc3edb-2d30-5056-8f79-133af7c7fe01" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-181484/selection/e432f1020975ada4c83798ae75bfd6c0ae4dfe63215763f08b8be8ccabf8a5d3" :answer/id #uuid "82a4b58d-d850-59c2-8d3e-d28fe1b9b527" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-181484/selection/6a47369bccbf2ed1a49e596d056fd7d8dad88dd2a1481ebd8897e05815dcfb4c" :answer/id #uuid "fb8112c7-4fa9-5b07-9a18-573429d79dab" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$A,B,$ and $C$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-181484/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "1e02b023-1826-5005-8bb3-c97a00eacefb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-181484/selection/e432f1020975ada4c83798ae75bfd6c0ae4dfe63215763f08b8be8ccabf8a5d3" :answer-field/choices ["q-181484/selection/850e7d7e17af59c82782dba2babdfd38f04745309ab27f16b30ef45ba075680e" "q-181484/selection/e956c3af673c94782038951d8de50bf89c9a53bf4daf2ca6725a4bea4229074c" "q-181484/selection/9ed3305a02906a861616af6ff886306c4a2a74885d99f506419451b33c7c9d2d" "q-181484/selection/e432f1020975ada4c83798ae75bfd6c0ae4dfe63215763f08b8be8ccabf8a5d3" "q-181484/selection/6a47369bccbf2ed1a49e596d056fd7d8dad88dd2a1481ebd8897e05815dcfb4c"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-181484" :question/id #uuid "518060a1-450f-5202-9fc1-024e53f2f2d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-181484" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡n=1}$\n- $B={q\\in Q\\,:\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡q=1}$\n- $C={x\\in R\\,:\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=1}$\n\nHint: The arguments of the trigonometric functions are in radians." :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\nWe start by writing down the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{sin}⁡x=1$ for $x\\in R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2m\\pi ,\\,m\\in Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is finite. There are no values of $m\\in Z$ such that $\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2m\\pi \\in Z.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nA={}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|A|=0,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $B$ is finite. Similar to the above, there are no values of $m\\in Z$ such that $\\frac{\\pi }{2}+2m\\pi \\in Q.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nB={}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|B|=0,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Listing the elements of $C$ for different values of $m,$ we have\n\n$$\nC={…,-\\frac{3\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{5\\pi }{2},\\frac{9\\pi }{2},…}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$C$ only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-181484/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f98332fd-e902-5390-a4d3-4e181ce97219"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-181484"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-181672/selection/850e7d7e17af59c82782dba2babdfd38f04745309ab27f16b30ef45ba075680e" :answer/id #uuid "1d406c81-917c-5a21-98c9-9126952fb8e7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the sets are infinite." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-181672/selection/f073cec77042b855fcc95ae59e5c07a0d566930857e27de8effde7dcda1f144f" :answer/id #uuid "74f0179d-bf6b-55b4-8625-2571962ba0dd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$A,$ $B,$ and $C$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-181672/selection/9ed3305a02906a861616af6ff886306c4a2a74885d99f506419451b33c7c9d2d" :answer/id #uuid "3108294b-b0be-5708-a8df-e059a0f5b3ae" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-181672/selection/e956c3af673c94782038951d8de50bf89c9a53bf4daf2ca6725a4bea4229074c" :answer/id #uuid "b08ddf3f-2488-58d1-9a9d-756f71d08af2" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-181672/selection/e432f1020975ada4c83798ae75bfd6c0ae4dfe63215763f08b8be8ccabf8a5d3" :answer/id #uuid "fd2fb6e2-e629-5434-b873-3a04a8b94141" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-181672/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "3049c2cb-22bd-513f-bf1b-8398c47d2876" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-181672/selection/e432f1020975ada4c83798ae75bfd6c0ae4dfe63215763f08b8be8ccabf8a5d3" :answer-field/choices ["q-181672/selection/850e7d7e17af59c82782dba2babdfd38f04745309ab27f16b30ef45ba075680e" "q-181672/selection/f073cec77042b855fcc95ae59e5c07a0d566930857e27de8effde7dcda1f144f" "q-181672/selection/9ed3305a02906a861616af6ff886306c4a2a74885d99f506419451b33c7c9d2d" "q-181672/selection/e956c3af673c94782038951d8de50bf89c9a53bf4daf2ca6725a4bea4229074c" "q-181672/selection/e432f1020975ada4c83798ae75bfd6c0ae4dfe63215763f08b8be8ccabf8a5d3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-181672" :question/id #uuid "91d1ceaa-e330-5897-8fd9-b4a90c8e955d" :question/math-academy-id "q-181672" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A={n\\in Z\\,:\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡n=1}$\n- $B={q\\in Q\\,:\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡q=1}$\n- $C={x\\in R\\,:\\,\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=1}$\n\nHint: The arguments of the trigonometric functions are in radians." :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\nWe start by writing down the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{tan}⁡x=1$ for $x\\in R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{\\pi }{4}+m\\pi ,\\,m\\in Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is finite. There are no values of $m\\in Z$ such that $\\frac{\\pi }{4}+m\\pi \\in Z.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nA={}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|A|=0,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $B$ is finite. Similar to the above, there are no values of $m\\in Q$ such that $\\frac{\\pi }{4}+m\\pi \\in Q.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nB={}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|B|=0,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Listing the elements of $C$ for different values of $m,$ we have\n\n$$\nC={…,-\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{5\\pi }{4},\\frac{9\\pi }{4},…}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$C$ only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-181672/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f98332fd-e902-5390-a4d3-4e181ce97219"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-181672"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "q-181668"] :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements. \n\nWe start by writing down the solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=0$ for $x\\in R:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}+m\\pi ,\\,m\\in Z\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is finite. There are no values of $m\\in Z$ such that $\\frac{\\pi }{2}+m\\pi \\in Z.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nA={}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|A|=0,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $B$ is finite. Similar to the above, there are no values of $m\\in Z$ such that $\\frac{\\pi }{2}+m\\pi \\in Q.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nB={}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|B|=0,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Listing the elements of $C$ for different values of $m,$ we have\n\n$$\nC={…,-\\frac{3\\pi }{2},-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{3\\pi }{2},…}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$C$ only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "q-181485/selection/e432f1020975ada4c83798ae75bfd6c0ae4dfe63215763f08b8be8ccabf8a5d3" :answer/id #uuid "278cb1d6-e25d-59d9-81fc-fd742e174518" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$C$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-181485/selection/9ed3305a02906a861616af6ff886306c4a2a74885d99f506419451b33c7c9d2d" :answer/id #uuid "454f8c24-193c-54b1-887c-fed13342213c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-181485/selection/d2c2cef76abecf21ba6ef213c7dc6d804b4da6aaffd800e2ad70a46a0a890800" :answer/id #uuid "f912101c-49f0-51d2-a424-0aba8dd9c9da" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$A,B$ and $C$" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-181485/selection/850e7d7e17af59c82782dba2babdfd38f04745309ab27f16b30ef45ba075680e" :answer/id #uuid "3cbc9be5-3510-5e55-8e52-e718a8ad59a1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the sets are infinite." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-181485/selection/685c02320dbe687345f93d820499dea4c0c82808b910e8a0e5841f77aa82209d" :answer/id #uuid "c785eb50-7d42-5e13-9a45-f93e2aaf3677" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-181485/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "f9cda6b1-9792-5afd-8566-a3c4fda5d5dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-181485/selection/e432f1020975ada4c83798ae75bfd6c0ae4dfe63215763f08b8be8ccabf8a5d3" :answer-field/choices ["q-181485/selection/e432f1020975ada4c83798ae75bfd6c0ae4dfe63215763f08b8be8ccabf8a5d3" "q-181485/selection/9ed3305a02906a861616af6ff886306c4a2a74885d99f506419451b33c7c9d2d" "q-181485/selection/d2c2cef76abecf21ba6ef213c7dc6d804b4da6aaffd800e2ad70a46a0a890800" "q-181485/selection/850e7d7e17af59c82782dba2babdfd38f04745309ab27f16b30ef45ba075680e" "q-181485/selection/685c02320dbe687345f93d820499dea4c0c82808b910e8a0e5841f77aa82209d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-181485" :question/id #uuid "d491cdc7-6d44-5ab0-bed5-c4e5b8db0525" :question/math-academy-id "q-181485" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A={{f}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}({e}^{x})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n- $B={{g}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(2{e}^{x})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n- $C={{h}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}({e}^{2x})\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$" :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\n- The set $A$ is finite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{(0)}(x) & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})={e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})={e}^{x} \\\\ {f}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{x})={e}^{x} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $A$ contains only one element:\n\n$$\nA={{e}^{x}}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|A|=1,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $B$ is finite. Similar to the previous case, $B$ contains only one element:\n\n$$\nA={2{e}^{x}}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|B|=1,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{(0)}(x) & ={e}^{2x} \\\\ {h}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({e}^{2x})=2{e}^{2x} \\\\ {h}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{e}^{2x})=4{e}^{2x} \\\\ {h}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4{e}^{2x})=8{e}^{2x} \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nC={{e}^{2x},2{e}^{2x},4{e}^{2x},8{e}^{2x},…}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$C$ only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-181485/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "26758016-ed47-562a-9338-85c71ffbeaa9"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-181485"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "q-181653/selection/b967c3002b3f913c5b31990d1f8159d3e1e979aa07e2842a8b67bfff1a2fd3f2" :answer/id #uuid "19b5f012-e343-5fc6-9441-f3bf80b2a2ae" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$A$ and $B$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-181653/selection/850e7d7e17af59c82782dba2babdfd38f04745309ab27f16b30ef45ba075680e" :answer/id #uuid "4f9b0c5a-db43-5b12-ac6a-47b16084b5f4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "None of the sets are infinite." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-181653/selection/9ed3305a02906a861616af6ff886306c4a2a74885d99f506419451b33c7c9d2d" :answer/id #uuid "1d3b93ec-49f0-52ee-afb8-cecb6f5135d5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$B$ and $C$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-181653/selection/e956c3af673c94782038951d8de50bf89c9a53bf4daf2ca6725a4bea4229074c" :answer/id #uuid "053e77bf-e50b-5568-bd30-e364fad105bb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$B$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-181653/selection/685c02320dbe687345f93d820499dea4c0c82808b910e8a0e5841f77aa82209d" :answer/id #uuid "9e93274d-0be8-5716-ade7-dc7d957b0a60" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "$A$ only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "q-181653/selection" :answer-field/id #uuid "90457a52-5b69-5649-9340-dca7ee296709" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "q-181653/selection/9ed3305a02906a861616af6ff886306c4a2a74885d99f506419451b33c7c9d2d" :answer-field/choices ["q-181653/selection/b967c3002b3f913c5b31990d1f8159d3e1e979aa07e2842a8b67bfff1a2fd3f2" "q-181653/selection/850e7d7e17af59c82782dba2babdfd38f04745309ab27f16b30ef45ba075680e" "q-181653/selection/9ed3305a02906a861616af6ff886306c4a2a74885d99f506419451b33c7c9d2d" "q-181653/selection/e956c3af673c94782038951d8de50bf89c9a53bf4daf2ca6725a4bea4229074c" "q-181653/selection/685c02320dbe687345f93d820499dea4c0c82808b910e8a0e5841f77aa82209d"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "q-181653" :question/id #uuid "78ca106c-05dd-5bce-b925-98c2aa7a1ea0" :question/math-academy-id "q-181653" :question/problem "Which of the following sets are infinite?\n\n- $A={{f}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n- $B={{g}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$\n- $C={{h}^{(n)}(x)=\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{n}}{\\text{d}{x}^{n}}(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)\\,:\\,n\\in {N}_{0}}$" :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\n- The set $A$ is finite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{(0)}(x) & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{(3)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x \\\\ {f}^{(4)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡x)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, $A$ contains four elements:\n\n$$\nA={-\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,-\\operatorname{cos}⁡x,\\operatorname{sin}⁡x,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|A|=4,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $B$ is infinite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{(0)}(x) & =-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x \\\\ {g}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)=\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x \\\\ {g}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x)=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-2{csc}^{3}⁡x \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nB={-\\operatorname{csc}⁡x,\\operatorname{cot}⁡x\\operatorname{csc}⁡x,\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-2{csc}^{3}⁡x,…}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $B$ is infinite.\n\n- The set $C$ is also infinite. Listing the first few elements of our set, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{h}^{(0)}(x) & =\\operatorname{cot}⁡x \\\\ {h}^{(1)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\operatorname{cot}⁡x)=-{csc}^{2}⁡x \\\\ {h}^{(2)}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(-{csc}^{2}⁡x)=2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x{csc}^{2}⁡x \\\\ ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we can write our set as follows:\n\n$$\nC={\\operatorname{cot}⁡x,-{csc}^{2}⁡x,2\\operatorname{cot}⁡x{csc}^{2}⁡x,…}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ and $C$ only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["q-181653/selection"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id [:knowledge-point/id #uuid "26758016-ed47-562a-9338-85c71ffbeaa9"] :knowledge-point/questions ["q-181653"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id [:question/math-academy-id "e-13171"] :question/worked-solution "The cardinality of a finite set is the number of elements contained within it. A set is infinite if it has infinitely many elements.\n\nLet's start by analyzing the parabola $y=x^2-9$ for $x\\in\\mathbb R.$\n\nFirst, we find the roots of the parabola:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-9 & =0 \\\\ (x-3)(x+3) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the roots are $x=-3$ and $x=3,$ and we can graph the parabola as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14072312/assets/3c2fc2f1eeb1d4e92f7933a408dafe7c3a216aa3d899f4025c71407140d47de1.png)\n\nTherefore:\n\n- The solution to the inequality $x^2-9 \\geq 0$ for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ is \n\n$$\nx \\leq -3 \\quad\\text{or}\\quad x \\geq 3\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x \\in I_1 = \\left(-\\infty, -3\\right] \\cup \\left[3,\\infty\\right).$\n\n- Similarly, the solution to the inequality $x^2-9 \\leq 0$ for $x \\in \\mathbb{R}$ is \n\n$$\n-3 \\leq x \\leq 3\n$$\n\nand can be expressed as $x \\in I_2 = [-3,3].$\n\nWith these in mind, let's examine our sets:\n\n- The set $A$ is infinite. If $n \\in \\mathbb{Z},$ the solutions to the inequality $n^2-9 \\geq 0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval $I_1.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nA = \\{ \\ldots, -4, -3 \\} \\cup \\{ 3, 4, \\ldots \\}.\n$$\n\n- The set $B$ is finite. If $m \\in \\mathbb{Z},$ the solutions to the inequality $m^2-9 \\leq 0$ are all the integers that lie in the interval $I_2.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\nB = \\{0,\\pm 1,\\pm 2,\\pm 3\\}.\n$$\n\nConsequently, $|B| = 7,$ which is finite.\n\n- The set $C$ is infinite. If $q \\in \\mathbb{Q},$ the solutions to $q^2-9 \\leq 0$ are all the rational numbers that lie in the interval $I_2.$ Since any real interval of nonzero length contains infinitely many rational numbers, it follows that $C$ is infinite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$A$ and $C$ only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]}]
